WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.007 --> 00:00:09.009
[solemn music playing]

2
00:00:19.269 --> 00:00:24.733
[man 1] <i>It's late at night
in Oklahoma City, in April of 1990.</i>

3
00:00:25.817 --> 00:00:27.360
[indistinct radio chatter]

4
00:00:27.444 --> 00:00:31.114
<i>Two or three guys are in a truck
driving along the road.</i>

5
00:00:34.743 --> 00:00:37.245
<i>And they see some kind of debris.</i>

6
00:00:38.955 --> 00:00:41.166
- [engine stops]
- [vehicle door opens, closes]

7
00:00:42.459 --> 00:00:44.002
[suspenseful music playing]

8
00:00:48.631 --> 00:00:51.551
<i>And then they see off to the side,
there's a body.</i>

9
00:00:55.430 --> 00:00:58.808
And it appears to be a young woman,
a blonde-haired woman.

10
00:00:59.851 --> 00:01:01.686
[sirens wailing]

11
00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:03.605
<i>And they call an ambulance.</i>

12
00:01:07.108 --> 00:01:08.818
<i>She's rushed to the hospital,</i>

13
00:01:09.360 --> 00:01:12.238
<i>and her husband, Clarence,
eventually shows up.</i>

14
00:01:15.408 --> 00:01:19.162
<i>He says her name is Tonya Hughes,
that she's a stripper in Tulsa,</i>

15
00:01:19.245 --> 00:01:22.373
<i>and that they have
a young son named Michael.</i>

16
00:01:25.543 --> 00:01:27.003
<i>And he's much older</i>

17
00:01:27.087 --> 00:01:29.297
<i>and he's just kind of this weird guy.</i>

18
00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:36.554
<i>So, the doctors,
as they continue to examine her,</i>

19
00:01:36.638 --> 00:01:38.431
<i>they see old bruises.</i>

20
00:01:38.515 --> 00:01:39.849
<i>They see old injuries.</i>

21
00:01:40.475 --> 00:01:42.602
<i>There's something wrong with this picture.</i>

22
00:01:43.186 --> 00:01:45.230
[monitor beeping rapidly]

23
00:01:45.313 --> 00:01:47.065
<i>Ultimately, she passes away.</i>

24
00:01:47.148 --> 00:01:49.192
[flatlining]

25
00:01:50.276 --> 00:01:52.403
[pensive music playing]

26
00:01:52.487 --> 00:01:57.158
<i>The girls Tonya danced with
want to find her family.</i>

27
00:01:58.326 --> 00:02:01.496
<i>So they call this woman
and they tell her that her daughter died.</i>

28
00:02:01.579 --> 00:02:03.623
<i>And she says,
"What are you talking about?"</i>

29
00:02:03.706 --> 00:02:07.210
<i>"My daughter died 20 years ago.
She was only 18 months old."</i>

30
00:02:11.464 --> 00:02:15.760
<i>They realize that
who they just buried was not Tonya Hughes.</i>

31
00:02:18.221 --> 00:02:19.597
<i>So now they're asking,</i>

32
00:02:20.098 --> 00:02:21.098
<i>"What happened?"</i>

33
00:02:21.558 --> 00:02:22.976
<i>"And who the hell is she?"</i>

34
00:02:23.977 --> 00:02:25.979
[pensive music continues]

35
00:02:27.730 --> 00:02:28.773
[film whirring]

36
00:02:28.857 --> 00:02:30.859
[music continues]

37
00:02:40.577 --> 00:02:42.495
[music fades]

38
00:02:42.579 --> 00:02:44.581
[bottles clinking]

39
00:02:49.043 --> 00:02:53.590
I met Tonya at Passions
in the fall of 1989.

40
00:02:53.673 --> 00:02:55.466
[electro music playing]

41
00:02:57.635 --> 00:03:00.305
We became friends
because we were the babies of the group.

42
00:03:00.388 --> 00:03:02.223
[electro music continues]

43
00:03:06.144 --> 00:03:08.271
And so we just kind of clicked together.

44
00:03:11.232 --> 00:03:13.735
We weren't like
the biker-chick type girls.

45
00:03:13.818 --> 00:03:17.780
We were just kind of like the type of girl
that went to school, graduated.

46
00:03:17.864 --> 00:03:20.074
I was going to college at that point.

47
00:03:20.158 --> 00:03:22.076
And so we had a lot in common.

48
00:03:25.038 --> 00:03:26.915
I could tell she was smart.

49
00:03:26.998 --> 00:03:30.543
You know, constantly reading.
Always wanting to get more knowledge.

50
00:03:30.627 --> 00:03:32.629
We talked all the time.

51
00:03:32.712 --> 00:03:34.214
Tonya was my best friend.

52
00:03:34.797 --> 00:03:36.799
[indistinct chatter]

53
00:03:38.760 --> 00:03:41.971
[Karen] When I met Tonya,
I met her husband, Clarence.

54
00:03:42.055 --> 00:03:43.598
He was an older man,

55
00:03:43.681 --> 00:03:46.643
and they had a two-year-old son, Michael.

56
00:03:46.726 --> 00:03:48.853
[relaxing music playing]

57
00:03:48.937 --> 00:03:51.147
Her son meant everything to her.

58
00:03:51.981 --> 00:03:54.192
He was her world.

59
00:03:57.946 --> 00:04:02.075
He was always smiling and happy.
I mean, he loved her so much.

60
00:04:02.951 --> 00:04:05.078
[music continues]

61
00:04:09.082 --> 00:04:13.086
Every time I've seen them together,
Michael would be right next to his mother.

62
00:04:13.169 --> 00:04:15.838
He wouldn't be next to Clarence at all.

63
00:04:18.675 --> 00:04:21.344
I asked her,
"Why don't we take Michael to the zoo?"

64
00:04:22.262 --> 00:04:23.930
And she's like, "No, no, no, no."

65
00:04:26.599 --> 00:04:29.269
Michael couldn't go anywhere

66
00:04:30.228 --> 00:04:31.437
with her alone.

67
00:04:39.279 --> 00:04:41.948
When we use dressing rooms,
you see each other.

68
00:04:42.824 --> 00:04:43.908
She had bruises.

69
00:04:43.992 --> 00:04:45.785
The whole backside of her.

70
00:04:49.497 --> 00:04:52.792
She said she'd slipped and fallen,
but I know different.

71
00:04:53.710 --> 00:04:56.421
[sniffling] And it was bad.

72
00:04:56.504 --> 00:04:58.506
[tense music playing]

73
00:05:02.552 --> 00:05:03.594
And it got worse.

74
00:05:04.095 --> 00:05:05.638
It kept getting worse.

75
00:05:07.223 --> 00:05:09.475
One day, Tonya came and told me

76
00:05:09.559 --> 00:05:13.146
that Clarence had taken
a life insurance policy out on her.

77
00:05:14.731 --> 00:05:16.107
She was terrified.

78
00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:19.569
She really wanted to run,
but she had Michael

79
00:05:19.652 --> 00:05:22.488
and he kept him under lock and key.

80
00:05:23.323 --> 00:05:24.782
Very lock and key.

81
00:05:26.326 --> 00:05:29.329
I think she was scared
he was going to hurt her and him.

82
00:05:29.912 --> 00:05:33.541
And I don't think she could've gotten
Michael away from Clarence.

83
00:05:34.042 --> 00:05:35.710
She... She was stuck

84
00:05:36.919 --> 00:05:38.504
and didn't know how to get out.

85
00:05:38.588 --> 00:05:40.506
[siren wailing]

86
00:05:44.260 --> 00:05:48.014
[Karen] Clarence called me
on April 25th, 1990,

87
00:05:48.097 --> 00:05:50.892
and told me that she'd been involved

88
00:05:50.975 --> 00:05:53.144
in a hit-and-run in Oklahoma City.

89
00:05:53.227 --> 00:05:55.396
[siren wailing]

90
00:05:56.272 --> 00:05:58.274
[suspenseful music playing]

91
00:06:01.944 --> 00:06:04.906
She never told me
that they were going out of town.

92
00:06:11.162 --> 00:06:13.164
[vehicles passing by]

93
00:06:13.706 --> 00:06:15.541
Why shouldn't she tell me that?

94
00:06:19.796 --> 00:06:21.464
None of it felt right.

95
00:06:24.550 --> 00:06:26.844
[suspenseful music playing]

96
00:06:26.928 --> 00:06:30.973
[man 2] Tonya Hughes arrived, uh,
sometime after midnight.

97
00:06:31.057 --> 00:06:36.020
She had a medium-severe
closed-head injury.

98
00:06:36.104 --> 00:06:39.899
She needed to be placed in the ICU
and treated as best we could

99
00:06:39.982 --> 00:06:43.903
with intravenous fluids,
medications to control her blood pressure,

100
00:06:43.986 --> 00:06:47.031
and medications
to control the brain swelling.

101
00:06:49.242 --> 00:06:52.870
Now, Clarence told me
that she was in the ICU,

102
00:06:52.954 --> 00:06:55.581
and that she was not allowed any visitors.

103
00:06:55.665 --> 00:06:58.334
I wasn't supposed to go see her,
nobody was supposed to go see her,

104
00:06:58.418 --> 00:07:00.128
but I went up anyway.

105
00:07:04.632 --> 00:07:07.260
And that's when the nurses came in,

106
00:07:07.343 --> 00:07:10.596
and that's when they told me
that they felt this was foul play.

107
00:07:11.722 --> 00:07:13.891
The nurses told me
it wasn't a hit-and-run.

108
00:07:14.517 --> 00:07:17.270
But what caught my attention
was the scratch marks right here.

109
00:07:17.353 --> 00:07:19.272
Like, somebody's hand scratches.

110
00:07:20.481 --> 00:07:23.025
To me, it looked like
she had been in a fight.

111
00:07:23.109 --> 00:07:25.111
[monitor beeping]

112
00:07:26.446 --> 00:07:32.493
[Engles] She was in pretty good shape,
considering an auto-pedestrian accident.

113
00:07:32.577 --> 00:07:35.037
Her vital signs were stable.

114
00:07:35.955 --> 00:07:39.167
But she, uh, seemed
to have a sinking spell

115
00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.877
and it... it was a mystery to me.

116
00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.962
[monitor beeping rapidly]

117
00:07:44.505 --> 00:07:46.507
[flatlining]

118
00:07:47.300 --> 00:07:50.845
[Karen] I'd gotten home and the phone
had rang. It was the hospital.

119
00:07:52.013 --> 00:07:53.347
That she had passed.

120
00:07:54.223 --> 00:07:55.892
[somber music playing]

121
00:08:05.776 --> 00:08:08.196
She was 20 years old when she died.

122
00:08:10.323 --> 00:08:11.616
[sobbing]

123
00:08:18.206 --> 00:08:19.624
[birds chirping]

124
00:08:22.877 --> 00:08:25.588
And that's how we found out
her name wasn't her name.

125
00:08:26.088 --> 00:08:27.590
[somber music continues]

126
00:08:30.843 --> 00:08:32.970
Her name wasn't actually Tonya Hughes,

127
00:08:33.054 --> 00:08:36.015
and we didn't know
who she was at that point.

128
00:08:38.601 --> 00:08:41.604
That's why we only put "Tonya"
on her tombstone

129
00:08:45.441 --> 00:08:49.570
And I don't think Michael was safe
with Clarence at all. At all.

130
00:08:51.489 --> 00:08:53.824
The hospital asked me
about Michael's condition

131
00:08:53.908 --> 00:08:55.993
and said that he was not talking.

132
00:08:56.077 --> 00:08:57.703
And I go, "That's not him."

133
00:08:58.412 --> 00:09:01.749
And that's when they gave me
the information to go to DHS.

134
00:09:01.832 --> 00:09:05.253
DHS came and got Michael
and put him in a foster home.

135
00:09:05.753 --> 00:09:06.753
[film whirring]

136
00:09:09.590 --> 00:09:11.592
[gentle music playing]

137
00:09:15.930 --> 00:09:17.473
- There he is.
- That's it.

138
00:09:18.516 --> 00:09:21.477
- Uh-huh. Mickey Mouse Club.
- Yeah.

139
00:09:21.978 --> 00:09:24.605
We had to wear
the Mickey Mouse hat that day.

140
00:09:27.191 --> 00:09:30.611
We got Michael on May 1st of 1990.

141
00:09:30.695 --> 00:09:34.949
Michael's mom passed away the day before,

142
00:09:35.616 --> 00:09:38.202
and he had just celebrated
his second birthday.

143
00:09:40.913 --> 00:09:43.291
We got instructions from the caseworker

144
00:09:43.374 --> 00:09:46.335
that he's still on the bottle
and he can only have Pepsi.

145
00:09:47.086 --> 00:09:49.338
If you ever told him no,

146
00:09:50.006 --> 00:09:51.841
he would go into hysteria.

147
00:09:51.924 --> 00:09:53.759
He would lay on the floor,

148
00:09:53.843 --> 00:09:56.053
like right out there
on that ceramic floor,

149
00:09:56.137 --> 00:09:58.014
and he would bang his head on the floor.

150
00:09:58.097 --> 00:09:59.366
He'd raise his head that high...

151
00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:02.143
Yeah, to the point where we were afraid
he was gonna injure himself.

152
00:10:02.226 --> 00:10:04.937
It was messed up in a way,
but he was a good kid.

153
00:10:08.566 --> 00:10:11.277
[Merle] The very first night
I took him off the Pepsi.

154
00:10:11.360 --> 00:10:12.903
Took him off the bottle.

155
00:10:14.447 --> 00:10:16.866
And just gave him a cup with milk in it.

156
00:10:17.408 --> 00:10:20.494
And so, you know, I thought,
"Man, what am I doing?"

157
00:10:20.578 --> 00:10:22.663
You know,
when you take a baby off a bottle,

158
00:10:22.747 --> 00:10:25.124
they're not too happy
the first few nights.

159
00:10:27.335 --> 00:10:29.420
Well, he started to settle.

160
00:10:30.463 --> 00:10:32.006
By the end of that week...

161
00:10:32.632 --> 00:10:35.217
- [Ernest] ...he's a different kid.
- ...he's a different kid.

162
00:10:36.636 --> 00:10:41.057
I never thought I'd see the day
that he would do that.

163
00:10:41.724 --> 00:10:44.018
We get Michael for four years.

164
00:10:44.685 --> 00:10:46.771
Just over the first two years,

165
00:10:46.854 --> 00:10:52.985
he actually started to grow
emotionally, physically, mentally.

166
00:10:53.069 --> 00:10:55.363
[happy music playing]

167
00:10:56.822 --> 00:10:59.492
The... The whole time we had Michael

168
00:11:00.242 --> 00:11:03.079
his dad had tried to get Michael back.

169
00:11:04.830 --> 00:11:07.541
[Clarence] My son is punished
for wanting to see his dad.

170
00:11:08.125 --> 00:11:10.002
Locked in his room,
his toys are taken away,

171
00:11:10.086 --> 00:11:13.339
and he told...
he's told that his dad's a mean man.

172
00:11:13.923 --> 00:11:17.426
We cannot allow removal of child
from a non-cruel and abusive home

173
00:11:17.510 --> 00:11:21.222
due to a wet diaper
or perceived lack of affection,

174
00:11:22.014 --> 00:11:25.226
which I was unable to give my son
during a period of a weak time

175
00:11:25.309 --> 00:11:27.269
when my wife died tragically.

176
00:11:28.646 --> 00:11:30.648
[Ernest] Now, he had visiting rights.

177
00:11:30.731 --> 00:11:33.150
Michael was made to go for visits.

178
00:11:33.234 --> 00:11:34.568
Michael didn't like that.

179
00:11:34.652 --> 00:11:38.989
We would tell Michael
that he had to go see his dad.

180
00:11:40.074 --> 00:11:43.661
He would crawl up under the piano bench
and he just kept saying,

181
00:11:43.744 --> 00:11:46.914
"That mean man.
That mean man. That mean man."

182
00:11:47.707 --> 00:11:50.251
It wasn't a good day for Michael, ever.

183
00:11:53.003 --> 00:11:55.131
[Clarence] Let me leave this thought
with you.

184
00:11:55.214 --> 00:11:56.882
Broken homes and cruel things

185
00:11:56.966 --> 00:12:00.469
perpetrated on children
cause a criminal to be born.

186
00:12:00.553 --> 00:12:02.555
[ominous music plays]

187
00:12:03.848 --> 00:12:06.851
It was assumed by everybody

188
00:12:06.934 --> 00:12:09.186
- that he was the father.
- ...that he was the dad.

189
00:12:09.687 --> 00:12:12.815
DHS called me and said that, um,

190
00:12:13.357 --> 00:12:16.444
we needed to take Michael
for a paternity test.

191
00:12:16.527 --> 00:12:18.988
The paternity test came about

192
00:12:19.071 --> 00:12:21.657
and it was proven
he was not the father in any way.

193
00:12:21.741 --> 00:12:23.451
No... No biological relation.

194
00:12:23.534 --> 00:12:25.536
[suspenseful music playing]

195
00:12:27.538 --> 00:12:31.250
[Merle] And they terminated
his parental rights.

196
00:12:32.001 --> 00:12:34.211
So then the visits stopped.

197
00:12:34.295 --> 00:12:36.380
So that same week,

198
00:12:36.464 --> 00:12:40.050
this pickup truck came by

199
00:12:40.134 --> 00:12:43.888
and this man is going, like,
five or ten miles an hour.

200
00:12:43.971 --> 00:12:46.056
He just kept looking at me the whole time.

201
00:12:46.140 --> 00:12:48.225
I'm thinking, "What's the man looking at?"

202
00:12:48.726 --> 00:12:50.561
I had this hunch. I don't know what.

203
00:12:51.228 --> 00:12:53.814
And I called DHS and I said,

204
00:12:53.898 --> 00:12:58.444
"Can you tell me what kind of vehicle
Clarence Hughes drives

205
00:12:58.527 --> 00:12:59.653
or what he looks like?"

206
00:12:59.737 --> 00:13:04.283
And when they described him to me, I said,
"That man was just by my house."

207
00:13:04.366 --> 00:13:07.119
Then they said, "You're just paranoid."
And I said,

208
00:13:07.203 --> 00:13:10.831
"No, I really feel like
something's going on."

209
00:13:12.082 --> 00:13:14.084
[ominous music playing]

210
00:13:18.339 --> 00:13:20.591
[man 3] On September 12th, 1994,

211
00:13:20.674 --> 00:13:24.345
we got the call that there was a man
had been tied to a tree in the woods,

212
00:13:24.428 --> 00:13:26.055
handcuffed, duct-taped.

213
00:13:26.138 --> 00:13:27.807
[music continues]

214
00:13:31.602 --> 00:13:34.939
The suspect casually walked in.
He asked to see the principal.

215
00:13:35.022 --> 00:13:36.816
He said he was there to get his son.

216
00:13:36.899 --> 00:13:39.735
He actually pulled a gun
partially out of his pocket.

217
00:13:39.819 --> 00:13:44.365
And said, "I wanna get Michael,
and you're gonna help me do that."

218
00:13:44.448 --> 00:13:46.951
He told me that he was ready to die.

219
00:13:47.034 --> 00:13:49.537
If I didn't help him,
I wasn't going to live.

220
00:13:51.288 --> 00:13:53.415
[Billy] And then he walked
into the classroom,

221
00:13:53.499 --> 00:13:55.167
with the principal at gunpoint

222
00:13:55.751 --> 00:13:59.421
and kidnapped Michael from the school,
along with the principal.

223
00:14:00.339 --> 00:14:03.259
He said, "We're gonna walk out here,
we're gonna take your truck."

224
00:14:03.843 --> 00:14:06.887
"And I want you to drive
a little ways to a dirt road."

225
00:14:08.013 --> 00:14:09.849
They stopped eventually.

226
00:14:09.932 --> 00:14:12.226
The principal was walked
into the wood line

227
00:14:12.768 --> 00:14:15.229
where he was handcuffed to a tree

228
00:14:15.312 --> 00:14:17.565
and then duct-taped
around his face and mouth.

229
00:14:18.858 --> 00:14:20.609
Eventually, after a period of time,

230
00:14:20.693 --> 00:14:23.404
someone found him
and called the authorities.

231
00:14:25.656 --> 00:14:28.951
After we cleared the scene,
we went ahead and contacted the FBI.

232
00:14:30.619 --> 00:14:35.583
The next day, agent Joe Fitzpatrick
came to my office, introduced himself.

233
00:14:35.666 --> 00:14:37.668
[pensive music playing]

234
00:14:38.586 --> 00:14:40.212
[objects clattering]

235
00:14:40.296 --> 00:14:42.298
[bell tinkling]

236
00:14:42.381 --> 00:14:45.676
[Joe] You fight more crime
in New York City on the way home

237
00:14:45.759 --> 00:14:47.761
than you do here in Oklahoma City.

238
00:14:49.555 --> 00:14:51.974
We don't have many kidnappings
in Oklahoma,

239
00:14:52.057 --> 00:14:55.394
but if we had a kidnapping,
I knew it was coming to me.

240
00:14:56.353 --> 00:14:59.481
In kidnappings, you wanna make
the recovery in the first 48 hours.

241
00:14:59.565 --> 00:15:02.484
If you don't,
your chances go down drastically.

242
00:15:04.236 --> 00:15:07.656
The very first thing we did
was put out an all-points bulletin.

243
00:15:08.532 --> 00:15:12.369
All the agencies in Oklahoma
were on the lookout for a young boy

244
00:15:12.453 --> 00:15:13.913
by the name of Michael Hughes,

245
00:15:13.996 --> 00:15:18.000
who had been taken by a person
claiming to be the boy's father,

246
00:15:18.083 --> 00:15:19.460
Clarence Hughes.

247
00:15:22.755 --> 00:15:25.799
We pulled his file and saw that in 1990,

248
00:15:25.883 --> 00:15:29.094
he tried to collect
on his wife's life insurance policy.

249
00:15:29.678 --> 00:15:34.892
But the social security number he gave
was for a man named Franklin Floyd.

250
00:15:35.768 --> 00:15:40.022
So Clarence Hughes's real name
was Franklin Delano Floyd.

251
00:15:40.105 --> 00:15:42.107
[dramatic music playing]

252
00:15:42.900 --> 00:15:45.527
Franklin Floyd had so many aliases.

253
00:15:48.238 --> 00:15:51.492
Trenton Davis, Warren Marshall,
and Clarence Hughes.

254
00:15:52.451 --> 00:15:56.622
We also realized that Franklin Floyd
had been in a halfway house.

255
00:15:56.705 --> 00:15:59.291
The fact he's in a halfway house
means he came out of prison.

256
00:15:59.375 --> 00:16:01.961
So I immediately pulled
his criminal record.

257
00:16:03.671 --> 00:16:05.923
In 1962, he abducted a girl.

258
00:16:07.466 --> 00:16:10.052
He robbed a bank in 1963.

259
00:16:10.678 --> 00:16:13.931
He was released
into a halfway house in 1972.

260
00:16:14.515 --> 00:16:17.476
Then in 1973, he attacked a female.

261
00:16:18.602 --> 00:16:20.896
He was arrested, posted bail,

262
00:16:20.980 --> 00:16:23.399
but then he failed
to appear in court for trial.

263
00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:29.029
He'd been on the run ever since that time,
and a fugitive for almost two decades.

264
00:16:30.406 --> 00:16:31.699
All this information together,

265
00:16:31.782 --> 00:16:34.451
very simple to analyze
that we had a real problem.

266
00:16:35.953 --> 00:16:39.623
[newsreader 1] <i>Franklin Floyd spent
ten years in prison in the '60s.</i>

267
00:16:39.707 --> 00:16:42.751
<i>After getting out,
he didn't report to his parole officer</i>

268
00:16:42.835 --> 00:16:45.838
<i>and was missing for the next 17 years.</i>

269
00:16:45.921 --> 00:16:47.921
[newsreader 2] <i>He was an expert
in being on the run</i>

270
00:16:47.965 --> 00:16:50.134
<i>and knew how to conceal his identity.</i>

271
00:16:50.217 --> 00:16:52.219
[pensive music playing]

272
00:16:56.390 --> 00:16:59.727
And there were real questions
about the boy's mother,

273
00:16:59.810 --> 00:17:01.895
who had died four years before.

274
00:17:01.979 --> 00:17:05.441
[newsreader 2] <i>The finger of blame
started pointing at Franklin Delano Floyd</i>

275
00:17:05.524 --> 00:17:09.153
<i>when co-workers of Tonya's,
from a Tulsa strip joint called Passions,</i>

276
00:17:09.236 --> 00:17:10.779
<i>went to police with their story.</i>

277
00:17:10.863 --> 00:17:12.281
[ominous music playing]

278
00:17:13.615 --> 00:17:18.662
When I first learned that Tonya was
actually killed by a hit-and-run driver,

279
00:17:18.746 --> 00:17:21.498
it was more than likely
Franklin Floyd had committed that.

280
00:17:22.750 --> 00:17:26.128
I mean, this guy was gonna be violent.
Catching him might be tricky.

281
00:17:26.211 --> 00:17:28.213
[music continues]

282
00:17:30.591 --> 00:17:32.760
Behavioral Science told me I had a week.

283
00:17:32.843 --> 00:17:36.597
Said after a week, Floyd would
most likely get tired of the boy,

284
00:17:36.680 --> 00:17:39.475
and he'd become a liability,
and he would kill him.

285
00:17:42.603 --> 00:17:44.998
[newsreader 3] <i>Floyd and young boy
are believed to be driving</i>

286
00:17:45.022 --> 00:17:47.858
<i>in a 1994 Ford pickup, full size.</i>

287
00:17:47.941 --> 00:17:52.571
<i>The tag number, QCN305,
Oklahoma license plate.</i>

288
00:17:53.989 --> 00:17:56.075
[newsreader 4] <i>Tonya Hughes
was the mother of the boy</i>

289
00:17:56.158 --> 00:17:57.910
<i>kidnapped from the Choctaw school,</i>

290
00:17:57.993 --> 00:18:02.122
<i>but she was run over by a car
in Oklahoma City in 1990.</i>

291
00:18:03.123 --> 00:18:04.958
[newsreader 5]
<i>Leads concerning Michael Hughes</i>

292
00:18:05.042 --> 00:18:08.670
<i>should be directed to the FBI at 842-7471.</i>

293
00:18:11.006 --> 00:18:17.179
In 1994, there was a big news report,
um, about the kidnapping.

294
00:18:17.763 --> 00:18:20.099
My mom was watching the news and she said,

295
00:18:20.182 --> 00:18:23.310
"Jenny, they have her under
a different name. Some name Tonya."

296
00:18:23.393 --> 00:18:25.938
"But that's Sharon. I'm telling you,
that's Sharon on the news."

297
00:18:26.021 --> 00:18:29.566
And I was like, "What's wrong?"
You know, "Is Sharon on the news?"

298
00:18:29.650 --> 00:18:31.944
"Are they interviewing her?
What's going on?"

299
00:18:32.027 --> 00:18:33.695
[sobbing] And my mom said, "No."

300
00:18:34.822 --> 00:18:36.490
"She is... Sharon's dead."

301
00:18:37.241 --> 00:18:38.241
[sniffling]

302
00:18:38.951 --> 00:18:42.204
I called the FBI.
I called the hotline and I said, you know,

303
00:18:43.122 --> 00:18:45.624
"There was a news report we had on about

304
00:18:46.708 --> 00:18:48.418
a girl named Tonya."

305
00:18:48.502 --> 00:18:49.586
"I know who she is."

306
00:18:49.670 --> 00:18:51.672
"Her name is Sharon Marshall."

307
00:18:52.339 --> 00:18:54.424
[sniffling] "She was
my high school best friend."

308
00:18:54.508 --> 00:18:55.634
[film whirring]

309
00:18:59.513 --> 00:19:01.014
[upbeat music playing]

310
00:19:10.315 --> 00:19:14.319
I met Sharon in 1984
at the student council camp.

311
00:19:15.445 --> 00:19:17.948
[woman] I was a classmate
of Sharon Marshall.

312
00:19:18.031 --> 00:19:21.451
We were in the gifted program together
at Forest Park Senior High.

313
00:19:22.536 --> 00:19:26.748
Sharon was in class of 86.
I was class of '87.

314
00:19:26.832 --> 00:19:29.960
I had two full years
with her being my best friend.

315
00:19:33.630 --> 00:19:35.424
It's a typical high school.

316
00:19:35.966 --> 00:19:39.219
[Sherry] Wild hallways, crazy kids.

317
00:19:39.303 --> 00:19:40.512
[whistling]

318
00:19:41.138 --> 00:19:43.765
People sneaking out
to the back parking lot to smoke.

319
00:19:45.142 --> 00:19:48.061
We would go to a mall
just to look for boys.

320
00:19:49.062 --> 00:19:52.065
We all had big hair and crazy clothes.

321
00:19:52.983 --> 00:19:58.197
It was just your normal
1980s high school kind of vibe.

322
00:20:02.618 --> 00:20:05.621
[Jenny] It all started
when I met Sharon at that summer camp.

323
00:20:05.704 --> 00:20:07.372
It was just instant best friends.

324
00:20:07.456 --> 00:20:10.459
I felt, from the first day,
I had known her my whole life.

325
00:20:12.878 --> 00:20:15.547
I remember her
the first day she came into that class

326
00:20:15.631 --> 00:20:18.592
because all the boys went, "Wow!" [laughs]

327
00:20:22.221 --> 00:20:25.474
She was just super smart. She was in ROTC.

328
00:20:26.558 --> 00:20:29.853
She was in science club.
She was in the gifted program.

329
00:20:30.354 --> 00:20:32.105
She had a lot going on for her.

330
00:20:35.984 --> 00:20:38.403
Sharon's friends were the outcasts.

331
00:20:39.196 --> 00:20:42.407
We weren't really all that popular.
We were the gifted kids.

332
00:20:42.491 --> 00:20:45.744
You know, the gifted kids are
not necessarily usually the popular ones.

333
00:20:45.827 --> 00:20:46.828
[chuckles]

334
00:20:47.454 --> 00:20:50.374
"Sherry, your yearbook's as bad as mine
to find a place to sign."

335
00:20:50.457 --> 00:20:52.960
"You're a really sweet and strange girl."

336
00:20:53.043 --> 00:20:54.253
"Stay that way."

337
00:20:54.336 --> 00:20:56.838
"Keep your unique style and be yourself."

338
00:20:57.422 --> 00:20:58.840
"Love you, Sharon Marshall."

339
00:21:01.718 --> 00:21:03.428
She always went for the underdogs.

340
00:21:04.930 --> 00:21:06.556
Just the kind of person she was.

341
00:21:08.934 --> 00:21:10.774
[Lynn] High school, for me,
was a little tough.

342
00:21:10.852 --> 00:21:12.938
I was smaller than the other guys.

343
00:21:13.021 --> 00:21:14.648
I was involved in art.

344
00:21:15.565 --> 00:21:17.901
Being almost beat up, being called a fag.

345
00:21:18.026 --> 00:21:19.152
That was normal.

346
00:21:20.821 --> 00:21:23.991
And my sophomore and junior years
were only made happy

347
00:21:24.074 --> 00:21:25.784
due to a couple of people.

348
00:21:26.618 --> 00:21:28.870
And one of those people
was Sharon Marshall.

349
00:21:30.831 --> 00:21:33.500
During school,
there was always a note for me

350
00:21:33.583 --> 00:21:35.210
that said, "Hey, Ray baby."

351
00:21:35.794 --> 00:21:38.797
"Hope you're having a good day.
I love you, Sharon."

352
00:21:43.135 --> 00:21:45.554
[Jenny] She was very mature for her age.

353
00:21:46.513 --> 00:21:50.892
She was very adamant from day one
that she wanted to go to Georgia Tech,

354
00:21:50.976 --> 00:21:53.145
and she wanted to be
an aerospace engineer.

355
00:21:54.646 --> 00:21:58.442
I remember the phone call when she called
and she was screaming on the phone.

356
00:21:58.525 --> 00:22:00.068
"I did it. I got in."

357
00:22:00.152 --> 00:22:01.778
"I got accepted to Georgia Tech,

358
00:22:01.862 --> 00:22:03.697
and I got a full scholarship,

359
00:22:03.780 --> 00:22:06.700
and I'm gonna be
in the aerospace engineering department."

360
00:22:06.783 --> 00:22:11.121
She was so excited.
It was the best day of her life.

361
00:22:11.204 --> 00:22:15.042
And she was so, so happy.
It's all she talked about.

362
00:22:16.752 --> 00:22:19.421
Her dad bought a full-page ad
in the yearbook

363
00:22:19.504 --> 00:22:22.382
to congratulate her
on the scholarship at Georgia Tech.

364
00:22:22.466 --> 00:22:24.468
Dad said, "Lookout, Georgia Tech!"

365
00:22:25.052 --> 00:22:29.431
"Hello, future Air Force General
and aeronautical engineering career. Dad."

366
00:22:36.938 --> 00:22:38.940
There were parents
who took out full-page ads

367
00:22:39.024 --> 00:22:42.194
but they were all baby pictures
and, you know...

368
00:22:43.236 --> 00:22:46.490
Why would a dad
choose something sexy for his daughter?

369
00:22:47.908 --> 00:22:49.659
That doesn't make any sense.

370
00:22:51.286 --> 00:22:53.997
We just thought her dad
was really strict and really weird.

371
00:22:56.416 --> 00:23:00.754
[Jenny] The first time I met her father
was when he brought Sharon up to my house.

372
00:23:00.837 --> 00:23:03.382
He pulled my dad aside
and asked for a loan.

373
00:23:04.549 --> 00:23:07.052
And my dad said, "No way." [laughs]

374
00:23:07.135 --> 00:23:08.136
You know, "No."

375
00:23:08.220 --> 00:23:11.348
And that put a real bad taste
in my parents' mouth.

376
00:23:11.431 --> 00:23:12.682
And that was day one.

377
00:23:15.018 --> 00:23:18.480
She told me
her mother was hit by a car on a bridge

378
00:23:18.563 --> 00:23:20.649
and died when she was in the second grade.

379
00:23:23.485 --> 00:23:26.196
She was in charge
of cooking dinner every night.

380
00:23:27.155 --> 00:23:30.409
Um, she wasn't... wasn't really allowed
to talk on the phone.

381
00:23:30.492 --> 00:23:33.870
She said, "Don't call here too much unless
I know in advance you're gonna call."

382
00:23:34.996 --> 00:23:36.289
"But I can call you."

383
00:23:36.873 --> 00:23:38.208
As soon as he would come home,

384
00:23:38.291 --> 00:23:40.585
I mean, she would get real anxious
in her voice

385
00:23:40.669 --> 00:23:42.379
and she's like, "Okay, okay, I got to go."

386
00:23:42.462 --> 00:23:43.742
You know? And she would hang up.

387
00:23:43.797 --> 00:23:44.923
[line disconnects]

388
00:23:47.384 --> 00:23:49.052
[phone ringing]

389
00:23:50.011 --> 00:23:52.139
And I remember the phone call
when she called,

390
00:23:52.222 --> 00:23:54.057
and she's bawling her eyes out,

391
00:23:54.141 --> 00:23:55.976
and she said she was pregnant.

392
00:23:57.811 --> 00:24:00.397
And I remember, um, being shocked.

393
00:24:00.480 --> 00:24:03.483
You know, crying
when she told me she was pregnant.

394
00:24:03.567 --> 00:24:05.887
And going, you know,
"Oh my God, what are you going to do?"

395
00:24:05.944 --> 00:24:08.572
[tense music playing]

396
00:24:08.655 --> 00:24:12.200
She's like, "Well, I'm gonna have it.
I'm gonna put him up for adoption."

397
00:24:13.702 --> 00:24:16.204
"But Daddy won't let me go
to college now."

398
00:24:17.831 --> 00:24:20.810
"You have to, you got a scholarship.
You're going to Tech. You're so smart."

399
00:24:20.834 --> 00:24:23.634
"You wanna be an aerospace engineer.
You're gonna do all these things."

400
00:24:23.670 --> 00:24:25.589
She said,
"Somebody has to take care of Daddy."

401
00:24:25.672 --> 00:24:26.715
[receiver clicks]

402
00:24:30.510 --> 00:24:34.347
[Lynn] I think it actually crushed her,
not being able to go to Georgia Tech,

403
00:24:34.431 --> 00:24:36.641
because that's all she worked for.

404
00:24:37.767 --> 00:24:39.769
I mean, that's all she worked for.

405
00:24:40.937 --> 00:24:42.939
[ominous music playing]

406
00:24:49.196 --> 00:24:51.114
Sharon called one day and said,

407
00:24:51.198 --> 00:24:52.958
"We're leaving in a coupla days
for Arizona."

408
00:24:56.578 --> 00:24:58.497
She said, "I'm gonna
have the baby out there."

409
00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:01.416
And she told me
she was gonna put him up for adoption.

410
00:25:01.500 --> 00:25:03.460
She said Daddy wouldn't let her keep it.

411
00:25:11.343 --> 00:25:13.345
[film whirring]

412
00:25:17.599 --> 00:25:20.101
[newsreader] <i>Police say
51-year-old Franklin Floyd</i>

413
00:25:20.185 --> 00:25:21.895
<i>entered the Indian Meridian...</i>

414
00:25:21.978 --> 00:25:24.689
[Walsh] <i>Twice now,
America's Most Wanted highlighted</i>

415
00:25:24.773 --> 00:25:26.149
<i>the Michael Hughes abduction.</i>

416
00:25:26.233 --> 00:25:30.237
<i>After last Saturday's episode, the show
got close to 20 calls from viewers</i>

417
00:25:30.320 --> 00:25:33.031
<i>who claim to know something
about the boy's whereabouts.</i>

418
00:25:33.114 --> 00:25:36.952
One of these tips is going to be <i>the tip</i>
that leads us to Michael Hughes.

419
00:25:38.328 --> 00:25:40.997
When Michael went missing,
it was all over the news.

420
00:25:41.081 --> 00:25:44.584
And I believe
that's when I met Joe Fitzpatrick.

421
00:25:44.668 --> 00:25:46.795
[suspenseful music playing]

422
00:25:50.298 --> 00:25:53.677
And, um, I sat and talked with him
for hours.

423
00:25:56.179 --> 00:26:01.351
And he said, "Jenny, not only is she dead,
but she's not who you think she is."

424
00:26:04.521 --> 00:26:07.399
[Joe] We showed her photographs
of Tonya Hughes.

425
00:26:07.983 --> 00:26:11.111
And she said
that was who she knew as Sharon Marshall.

426
00:26:14.322 --> 00:26:18.410
And that, uh, this man
who we knew as Clarence Hughes

427
00:26:18.493 --> 00:26:22.080
was who she knew
as Sharon's father, Warren Marshall.

428
00:26:24.332 --> 00:26:26.334
[Jenny] They thought this was his wife.

429
00:26:26.418 --> 00:26:28.712
They said, "They're married."

430
00:26:28.795 --> 00:26:33.049
And I'm like, "No, that's her father.
They're not married."

431
00:26:33.133 --> 00:26:35.176
And they said,
"No, honey, they're married."

432
00:26:35.260 --> 00:26:37.887
And I was like, "No. That was her father."

433
00:26:40.265 --> 00:26:45.437
We had a portrait of very different people
of who Sharon was.

434
00:26:51.276 --> 00:26:53.695
[Joe] We realized,
after talking to Jenny Fisher,

435
00:26:53.778 --> 00:26:56.239
that Tonya also had aliases.

436
00:26:56.781 --> 00:26:58.950
[suspenseful music playing]

437
00:26:59.034 --> 00:27:04.039
We discovered that,
in 1989, one year before her death,

438
00:27:04.122 --> 00:27:05.624
they changed their names.

439
00:27:06.416 --> 00:27:08.501
Sharon Marshall became Tonya,

440
00:27:08.585 --> 00:27:12.213
and Warren Marshall
became Clarence Hughes.

441
00:27:13.882 --> 00:27:18.553
And the names they used
were taken off tombstones in Alabama.

442
00:27:20.680 --> 00:27:24.184
And then they got married
under the new names, in New Orleans,

443
00:27:25.185 --> 00:27:27.979
which means
this man married his own daughter.

444
00:27:29.564 --> 00:27:30.982
It was unbelievable.

445
00:27:32.692 --> 00:27:34.194
One question led to another.

446
00:27:34.277 --> 00:27:36.863
And soon police were wondering
just who they were dealing with.

447
00:27:36.946 --> 00:27:39.699
<i>Investigation showed
she went by many names,</i>

448
00:27:39.783 --> 00:27:43.119
<i>like Tonya D. Hughes
and Tonya Dawn Tadlock.</i>

449
00:27:43.203 --> 00:27:45.622
<i>But who was this woman and who killed her?</i>

450
00:27:46.748 --> 00:27:47.874
There's a big question here.

451
00:27:47.957 --> 00:27:52.212
What happened to Sharon Marshall
in this time period between high school

452
00:27:52.295 --> 00:27:54.631
until she was found dead
on the side of the road?

453
00:27:54.714 --> 00:27:56.716
- [film whirring]
- [somber music playing]

454
00:27:59.803 --> 00:28:01.429
Her death was suspicious.

455
00:28:03.682 --> 00:28:06.309
But there was no proof
that Floyd killed her.

456
00:28:08.937 --> 00:28:10.897
What happened to Sharon Marshall?

457
00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:12.982
[music continues]

458
00:28:24.577 --> 00:28:25.787
[dramatic whoosh]

459
00:28:25.870 --> 00:28:27.872
[music fades]

460
00:28:30.542 --> 00:28:32.252
[Jenny] I heard from her one time.

461
00:28:32.335 --> 00:28:34.504
She told me, you know, "We're moving some,

462
00:28:34.587 --> 00:28:36.467
we're going to go out West.
We're doing this..."

463
00:28:36.548 --> 00:28:39.259
They, somehow, in all of that,
ended up down in Tampa.

464
00:28:42.554 --> 00:28:46.516
She was employed as a stripper
in one of the clubs there.

465
00:28:49.602 --> 00:28:52.647
[woman] Mons Venus, in the late 1980s,

466
00:28:52.731 --> 00:28:57.318
was the only place to go
for adult entertainment.

467
00:28:57.861 --> 00:28:59.612
[rock music playing]

468
00:29:03.575 --> 00:29:08.705
This was known as a place
that had the most beautiful girls

469
00:29:08.788 --> 00:29:09.914
in the world.

470
00:29:10.915 --> 00:29:13.543
We didn't just wear lingerie.

471
00:29:13.626 --> 00:29:17.046
We wore amazing French lingerie.

472
00:29:17.130 --> 00:29:18.923
We were the best of everything.

473
00:29:20.633 --> 00:29:23.887
We had everyone come through here.

474
00:29:24.429 --> 00:29:25.680
Women.

475
00:29:25.764 --> 00:29:27.182
Athletes.

476
00:29:27.265 --> 00:29:28.349
Musicians.

477
00:29:29.267 --> 00:29:30.310
Businessmen.

478
00:29:31.186 --> 00:29:33.188
